g two replicas but only one is ready. That could indicate an issue with the frontend service.\n\nBut the user hasn't checked the pods yet. Maybe the frontend has pods that are not ready. So the next step should be to check the pods in the hotel-reservation namespace to see their status. Using `kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation` would show if any pods are in CrashLoopBackOff, Error, or NotReady states. That would help identify if there are any faulty microservices. \n\nAlternatively, maybe checking the metrics or traces could help, but first, it's better to confirm the pod statuses. Since the deployments show some replicas not ready, checking the actual pods would give more details. So the next tool should be `exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd` with the command `kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation`.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The frontend deployment shows 1/2 READY replicas, indicating potential issues with one of its pods. To confirm if any pods are in a failed state (e.g., CrashLoopBackOff, Error, or NotReady), I need to inspect the pod list directly. This will reveal if specific microservices are malfunctioning and provide actionable insights for further diagnosis.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': Usage(completion_tokens=372, prompt_tokens=3127, total_tokens=3499, completion_tokens_details=None, prompt_tokens_details=None), 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbe2-b16b-78e0-bba4-78604bbaae8b-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3127, 'output_tokens': 372, 'total_tokens': 3499}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='58702bef-81c8-47c6-86cf-c5e750aee495')]]
