</tool_call> - base_agent.py:llm_thinking_step:68 [Full Prompt = [SystemMessage(content="Monitor and diagnose an application consisting of **MANY** microservices. Some or none of the microservices have faults. Get all the pods and deployments to figure out what kind of services are running in the cluster.  Carefully identify the whether the faults are present and if they are, and identify what is the root cause of the fault.\nStop diagnosis once you've found the root cause of the faults.\nGo as deep as you can into what is causing the issue.\nYour instructions to the tools must be clear and concise. Your queries to tools need to be single turn.\nRemember to check these, and remember this information: ## Workloads (Applications) - **Pod**: The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes, representing a single instance of a running application. Can contain one or more tightly coupled containers. - **ReplicaSet**: Ensures that a specified number of pod replicas are running at all times. Often managed indirectly through Deployments. - **Deployment**: Manages the deployment and lifecycle of applications. Provides declarative updates for Pods and ReplicaSets. - **StatefulSet**: Manages stateful applications with unique pod identities and stable storage. Used for workloads like databases. - **DaemonSet**: Ensures that a copy of a specific pod runs on every node in the cluster. Useful for node monitoring agents, log collectors, etc. - **Job**: Manages batch processing tasks that are expected to complete successfully. Ensures pods run to completion. - **CronJob**: Schedules jobs to run at specified times or intervals (similar to cron in Linux).\n## Networking - **Service**: Provides a stable network endpoint for accessing a group of pods. Types: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, and ExternalName. - **Ingress**: Manages external HTTP(S) access to services in the cluster. Supports routing and load balancing for HTTP(S) traffic. - **NetworkPolicy**: Defines rules for network communication between pods and other entities. Used for security and traffic control.\n## Storage - **PersistentVolume (PV)**: Represents a piece of storage in the cluster, provisioned by an administrator or dynamically. - **PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)**: Represents a request for storage by a user. Binds to a PersistentVolume. - **StorageClass**: Defines different storage tiers or backends for dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes. - **ConfigMap**: Stores configuration data as key-value pairs for applications. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive data like passwords, tokens, or keys in an encrypted format.\n## Configuration and Metadata - **Namespace**: Logical partitioning of resources within the cluster for isolation and organization. - **ConfigMap**: Provides non-sensitive configuration data in key-value format. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive configuration data securely. - **ResourceQuota**: Restricts resource usage (e.g., CPU, memory) within a namespace. - **LimitRange**: Enforces minimum and maximum resource limits for containers in a namespace.\n## Cluster Management - **Node**: Represents a worker machine in the cluster (virtual or physical). Runs pods and is managed by the control plane. - **ClusterRole and Role**: Define permissions for resources at the cluster or namespace level. - **ClusterRoleBinding and RoleBinding**: Bind roles to users or groups for authorization. - **ServiceAccount**: Associates processes in pods with permissions for accessing the Kubernetes API.\nAfter you finished, submit the detected faulty components to the orchestrator for evaluation. The submission should be a natural language description of the root cause of the failure.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='73f33109-e3a6-43f6-b5ac-21826dcb8a56'), HumanMessage(content="You will be working this application:\nHotel Reservation\nHere are some descriptions about the application:\nA hotel reservation application built with Go and gRPC, providing backend in-memory and persistent databases, a recommender system for hotel recommendations, and a functionality to place reservations.\nIt belongs to this namespace:\nhotel-reservation\nIn each round, there is a thinking stage. In the thinking stage, you are given a list of tools. Think about what you want to call. Return your tool choice and the reasoning behind When choosing the tool, refer to the tool by its name. Then, there is a tool-call stage, where you make a tool_call consistent with your explanation. You can run up to 20 rounds to finish the tasks. If you call submit_tool in tool-call stage, the process will end immediately. If you exceed this limitation, the system will force you to make a submission. You will begin by analyzing the service's state and telemetry with the tools.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='191fb2a5-e319-422b-8d7f-975c2b4ba30c'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Here are all the tools you can use:\ntool name: get_traces\n\ntool description: Get Jaeger traces for a given service in the last n minutes.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service for which to retrieve trace data.\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n\n\ntool name: get_services\n\ntool description: Retrieve the list of service names from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of service names available in Grafana.\n\n\ntool name: get_operations\n\ntool description: Query available operations for a specific service from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service whose operations should be retrieved.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of operation names associated with the specified service.\n\n\ntool name: get_dependency_graph\n\ntool description: Get service dependency graph from Jaeger\'s native dependencies API.\n    Args:\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n    Returns:\n        str: JSON object representing the dependency graph.\n\n\ntool name: get_metrics\n\ntool description: Query real-time metrics data from the Prometheus instance.\n\n    Args:\n        query (str): A Prometheus Query Language (PromQL) expression used to fetch metric values.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: The raw Prometheus response containing metric results, including timestamps, values, and labels.\n\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\n\ntool description: This is a tool used to execute read-only kubectl commands.\n\n    Args:\n        command (str): The read-only kubectl command you want to execute in a CLI to manage a k8s cluster. It should start with "kubectl".\n        Available Read-only Commands:\n        "kubectl api-resources",\n        "kubectl api-version",\n        "kubectl auth can-i",\n        "kubectl cluster-info",\n        "kubectl describe",\n        "kubectl diff",\n        "kubectl events",\n        "kubectl explain",\n        "kubectl get",\n        "kubectl logs",\n        "kubectl options",\n        "kubectl top",\n        "kubectl version",\n        "kubectl config view",\n        "kubectl config current-context",\n        "kubectl config get",\n        INTERACTIVE COMMANDS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g. "kubectl edit"\n        INTERACTIVE FLAGS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g., "kubectl logs -f"\n\n\ntool name: submit_tool\n\ntool description: The tool to submit benchmark results\n\n    Args:\n        ans (str): the answer you would like to submit to the benchmark. Use natural language to describe the root cause of the failure.\n\n\nChoose a tool from the list and output the tool name. Justify your tool choice. In the next step, you will generate a tool call for this tool', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='f77db898-b24b-4061-a3aa-7d4f75903bd6'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's start by understanding the problem. The user is dealing with a hotel reservation application composed of many microservices. The goal is to monitor and diagnose any faults in the system. The first step is to figure out what services are running in the cluster. Since the application is in the namespace 'hotel-reservation', I need to get a list of all the services within that namespace.\n\nLooking at the available tools, the 'get_services' tool from Grafana can retrieve the list of service names. However, the user mentioned that the application uses Kubernetes, so maybe checking the Kubernetes resources would be more direct. The 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd' tool allows running kubectl commands. Using 'kubectl get services' in the 'hotel-reservation' namespace would list all the services. But the user might not have access to the cluster's kubectl, or maybe the Grafana tool is more appropriate here. Wait, the 'get_services' tool is specific to Grafana, which might not list all Kubernetes services but rather services monitored in Grafana. However, the user hasn't specified if Grafana is part of the setup. \n\nAlternatively, using 'get_services' from Grafana might give the list of services that are being monitored, which could be the same as the Kubernetes services. But if the application has many microservices, it's crucial to know all the services first. Since the user hasn't provided specific info, maybe starting with 'get_services' is better. But if that's not available, perhaps using kubectl. However, the 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd' tool can run 'kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation' to list all services in that namespace. That would give the list of services, which is essential for further diagnosis. So, the first step is to get the list of services to identify which ones are part of the application. Therefore, the correct tool here is 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd' with the command 'kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation'.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\nreasoning: To identify all microservices running in the hotel-reservation namespace, I need to first list all services (pods, deployments, etc.) in the cluster. Using kubectl get services in the target namespace will provide the foundational list of services to investigate further. This is the first step in understanding the application's architecture and identifying potential fault points.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 496, 'prompt_tokens': 1500, 'total_tokens': 1996, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbe2-5433-7423-a112-3292da19ec23-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1500, 'output_tokens': 496, 'total_tokens': 1996}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b285377b-e386-461a-a998-4e0849273918'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me figure out the next step. The user wants me to monitor and diagnose a hotel reservation application with many microservices. The first thing I need to do is get a list of all the services running in the cluster, specifically in the hotel-reservation namespace. \n\nIn the previous step, I chose the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool. The reasoning was that using kubectl get services in the target namespace would provide the list of services. But wait, the user mentioned that the application consists of many microservices, so maybe I should check for pods, deployments, and other resources as well. However, the initial step is to get the services, which includes the names of the services. \n\nWait, the user provided a list of tools. The get_services tool retrieves service names from Grafana, but maybe that's not the same as the actual Kubernetes services. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd can run 'kubectl get services' in the hotel-reservation namespace. But the user might have a different setup. Let me check the available commands again. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd allows 'kubectl get' commands. So, the correct command would be 'kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation' to list all services in that namespace. \n\nBut the user might also need to check deployments, statefulsets, daemonsets, etc., but the first step is to get the services. So the tool call should be to exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command 'kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation'. That will give the list of services, which are the microservices in the application. Once I have the service names, I can proceed to check their metrics, traces, and dependencies. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the get_services tool from Grafana is more appropriate, but the user hasn't mentioned Grafana details. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd is more direct for Kubernetes resources. So I'll proceed with that.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9b0bb9feec505d53', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 428, 'prompt_tokens': 2487, 'total_tokens': 2915, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbe2-6ba9-7cc3-9e05-582e071e8dd3-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9b0bb9feec505d53', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2487, 'output_tokens': 428, 'total_tokens': 2915}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                     TYPE           CLUSTER-IP       EXTERNAL-IP                                PORT(S)                               AGE\nconsul                   ClusterIP      10.101.46.2      <none>                                     8300/TCP,8400/TCP,8500/TCP,8600/UDP   100s\nfrontend                 ClusterIP      10.100.17.55     <none>                                     5000/TCP                              100s\ngeo                      ClusterIP      10.109.224.104   <none>                                     8083/TCP                              99s\njaeger                   ExternalName   <none>           otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local   <none>                                24s\njaeger-agent             ExternalName   <none>           otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local   <none>                                24s\njaeger-collector         ExternalName   <none>           otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local   <none>                                24s\njaeger-out               NodePort       10.107.198.84    <none>                                     16686:31778/TCP                       96s\njaeger-query             ExternalName   <none>           otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local   <none>                                23s\nmemcached-profile        ClusterIP      10.96.48.17      <none>                                     11211/TCP                             93s\nmemcached-rate           ClusterIP      10.105.245.38    <none>                                     11211/TCP                             87s\nmemcached-reserve        ClusterIP      10.101.151.104   <none>                                     11211/TCP                             80s\nmongodb-geo              ClusterIP      10.104.60.200    <none>                                     27017/TCP                             98s\nmongodb-profile          ClusterIP      10.104.243.22    <none>                                     27017/TCP                             91s\nmongodb-rate             ClusterIP      10.97.109.0      <none>                                     27017/TCP                             86s\nmongodb-recommendation   ClusterIP      10.96.191.216    <none>                                     27017/TCP                             83s\nmongodb-reservation      ClusterIP      10.96.87.177     <none>                                     27017/TCP                             78s\nmongodb-user             ClusterIP      10.102.11.167    <none>                                     27017/TCP                             70s\nprofile                  ClusterIP      10.104.199.55    <none>                                     8081/TCP                              89s\nrate                     ClusterIP      10.108.161.53    <none>                                     8084/TCP                              84s\nrecommendation           ClusterIP      10.103.39.84     <none>                                     8085/TCP                              81s\nreservation              ClusterIP      10.97.190.123    <none>                                     8087/TCP                              75s\nsearch                   ClusterIP      10.106.14.101    <none>                                     8082/TCP                              72s\nuser                     ClusterIP      10.106.33.90     <none>                                     8086/TCP                              68s\n', id='09a6a04d-ab64-40e8-a8c3-1d90de3940cd', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-9b0bb9feec505d53'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='a4aa33d3-1c86-4430-a271-ff0804f289fd'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to monitor and diagnose a hotel reservation application with many microservices. The first step was to get the list of services using kubectl get services in the hotel-reservation namespace. The response showed a bunch of services like frontend, geo, memcached instances, MongoDB services, and others.\n\nNow, the next step is to figure out which of these services might be faulty. Since the user mentioned that some or none of the microservices have faults, I need to check for issues. The initial list gives service names, but I need more details on their health and performance. \n\nLooking at the available tools, get_metrics could be useful here. Prometheus is commonly used for metrics, so querying metrics like error rates, latency, or pod statuses might reveal problems. For example, if a service has high error rates or is unreachable, that\'s a sign of a fault. \n\nAlternatively, get_traces from Jaeger could help identify issues in the service dependencies. If there are traces showing delays or failures in specific services, that could point to the root cause. But first, maybe checking the metrics would give a broader overview. \n\nAnother option is get_dependency_graph to see how services are interconnected. If a service is down, it might affect others. But without knowing which service is faulty yet, starting with metrics makes sense. \n\nWait, the user also mentioned checking pods and deployments. Maybe using kubectl to get pods and their statuses would help. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd can run "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation" to check if any pods are in CrashLoopBackOff or similar states. That\'s a good next step. \n\nBut the user\'s previous step was to get services, not pods. So maybe the next step is to check the pods\' statuses. Let me think. If the services are up but pods are crashing, that\'s a problem. So using kubectl get pods would show if any pods are not running properly. \n\nAlternatively, using get_metrics with specific queries like "up" or "http_requests_total" could indicate if services are responding. But without knowing the exact metrics, it\'s a bit vague. \n\nHmm, the user might need to check both pods and metrics. But since the tools available include exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd, which can get pods, that\'s a good next step. So the next tool should be exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation" to check pod statuses. That would help identify if any pods are not running, which could be the root cause.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The previous step listed services, but to identify faulty microservices, I need to check the actual pod statuses and deployments. Running `kubectl get pods` in the namespace will reveal if any pods are in CrashLoopBackOff, Error, or NotReady states, which directly indicate runtime issues. This is critical for pinpointing failing components before diving into metrics or traces.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 618, 'prompt_tokens': 2567, 'total_tokens': 3185, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbe2-81f2-7290-9efa-502d44d92d9f-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2567, 'output_tokens': 618, 'total_tokens': 3185}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='bcbb4c19-510e-4550-96a9-b3945c64256e'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, the user is trying to diagnose faults in a hotel reservation application composed of many microservices. They started by listing the services using `kubectl get services`, which gave a list of services in the namespace. Then they moved to check the pods with `kubectl get pods` to see if any are in a bad state.\n\nThe next step is to check the deployments and replica sets to see if there are any issues with the desired vs. current replicas. If some pods are not running, the deployments might be stuck. So, using `kubectl get deployments` and `kubectl get replicasets` would help here. But since the user has a limited number of tool calls, they need to be efficient. \n\nLooking at the available tools, `exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd` is the way to go. The command should be `kubectl get deployments` to check the deployment statuses. If deployments are not healthy, that could indicate issues like image pull errors, configuration problems, or resource constraints. \n\nAlternatively, checking the pods' details with `kubectl describe pods` could provide more insights into why a pod is not running. But since the user wants to check deployments first, starting with `kubectl get deployments` makes sense. This will show if the deployments are stuck, which is a common issue. \n\nSo, the next tool call should be `kubectl get deployments` in the specified namespace to check deployment statuses. If that shows issues, then proceed to check the pods and their events for more details.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get deployments -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-995dc147a9c12023', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 336, 'prompt_tokens': 3558, 'total_tokens': 3894, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbe2-9e89-78b2-b537-f0df314d0b8f-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get deployments -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-995dc147a9c12023', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3558, 'output_tokens': 336, 'total_tokens': 3894}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                     READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   AGE\nconsul                   1/1     1            1           112s\nfrontend                 1/2     2            1           34s\ngeo                      1/1     1            1           111s\njaeger                   1/1     1            1           108s\nmemcached-profile        1/1     1            1           105s\nmemcached-rate           1/1     1            1           100s\nmemcached-reserve        1/1     1            1           92s\nmongodb-geo              1/1     1            1           110s\nmongodb-profile          1/1     1            1           103s\nmongodb-rate             1/1     1            1           98s\nmongodb-recommendation   1/1     1            1           95s\nmongodb-reservation      1/1     1            1           91s\nmongodb-user             1/1     1            1           83s\nprofile                  1/1     1            1           102s\nrate                     1/1     1            1           97s\nrecommendation           1/1     1            1           95s\nreservation              1/1     1            1           89s\nsearch                   1/1     1            1           85s\nuser                     1/1     1            1           81s\n', id='e933d150-732b-4dcb-93ad-915524e4f28a', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-995dc147a9c12023'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='685b9437-d1f2-4b71-916f-fd7e53255004'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose a hotel reservation application with many microservices. So far, they've checked the services and deployments. The services list showed some services like frontend, geo, jaeger, etc., and the deployments all show READY, UP-TO-DATE, AVAILABLE as 1/1, which seems okay. But wait, the frontend deployment has 1/2 READY. That might be a problem. Wait, the user ran `kubectl get deployments` and saw that frontend has 1/2, meaning two replicas but only one is ready. That could indicate an issue with the frontend service.\n\nBut the user hasn't checked the pods yet. Maybe the frontend has pods that are not ready. So the next step should be to check the pods in the hotel-reservation namespace to see their status. Using `kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation` would show if any pods are in CrashLoopBackOff, Error, or NotReady states. That would help identify if there are any faulty microservices. \n\nAlternatively, maybe checking the metrics or traces could help, but first, it's better to confirm the pod statuses. Since the deployments show some replicas not ready, checking the actual pods would give more details. So the next tool should be `exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd` with the command `kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation`.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The frontend deployment shows 1/2 READY replicas, indicating potential issues with one of its pods. To confirm if any pods are in a failed state (e.g., CrashLoopBackOff, Error, or NotReady), I need to inspect the pod list directly. This will reveal if specific microservices are malfunctioning and provide actionable insights for further diagnosis.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 372, 'prompt_tokens': 3127, 'total_tokens': 3499, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbe2-b16b-78e0-bba4-78604bbaae8b-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3127, 'output_tokens': 372, 'total_tokens': 3499}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='58702bef-81c8-47c6-86cf-c5e750aee495'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in the Hotel Reservation application, which consists of many microservices. So far, they\'ve used kubectl commands to list services and deployments. The frontend deployment shows 1/2 READY replicas, which is a red flag. That means one pod is not ready, possibly causing problems.\n\nThe next step is to check the actual pods to see their status. The user mentioned using kubectl get pods to find any CrashLoopBackOff, Error, or NotReady states. The previous command was kubectl get deployments, but that\'s not showing the individual pods. So, the correct command here would be kubectl get pods in the hotel-reservation namespace. \n\nWait, the user already ran kubectl get services and deployments. The deployments show frontend with 1/2 READY, but maybe the pods under frontend are the issue. So, running kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation will list all pods, including the frontend\'s pods. That way, they can check if any pods are in a bad state. \n\nI need to make sure the command is correct. The available commands include "kubectl get pods", so that\'s valid. The user needs to see the pod names and their statuses. If any pods are not ready, they can then check logs or metrics for those specific pods. \n\nSo the tool call should be exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation". That should provide the necessary information to identify faulty microservices.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-84b790e7d7620dc5', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 346, 'prompt_tokens': 4117, 'total_tokens': 4463, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbe2-c34d