s\nwrk2-job-lh2f2                            1/1     Running   0             45s\n', id='46c271bf-14d2-4fce-bac0-bc22c893f19d', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-84b790e7d7620dc5'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c8a94314-022b-4d4b-9d00-b0c466d0026c'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in the Hotel Reservation application, which has multiple microservices. So far, they've checked the services, deployments, and pods. The frontend deployment has two pods, but one is pending. The other pods seem to be running, but some have restarts or status issues.\n\nThe next step is to figure out why the frontend pod is pending. Pending usually means the pod hasn't been scheduled yet. Possible reasons could be resource constraints, node issues, or image pull problems. Since the user has access to kubectl, they can check the pod details for more info. Using 'kubectl describe pod' on the frontend pod might show events or errors related to scheduling. Also, checking the node resources to see if there's insufficient CPU or memory. Another angle is checking if the image is available or if there's a pull error. They might need to look into the pod's events and node status to pinpoint the cause. So, the next tool should be 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd' with the describe command on the frontend pod.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The frontend pod is in a Pending state, which suggests scheduling issues. To diagnose the root cause, I need to inspect the pod's detailed status, including events, resource constraints, or image pull failures. Using `kubectl describe pod` on the frontend pod will provide critical insights into why it cannot be scheduled, such as node resource limits, image availability, or taints. This is the next logical step to identify the specific failure condition.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 328, 'prompt_tokens': 4014, 'total_tokens': 4342, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbe2-d64b-7521-aa94-62c06eaa08e7-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4014, 'output_tokens': 328, 'total_tokens': 4342}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='33bed63d-3803-4c5d-a037-40f9eb105add'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with their hotel reservation application, which consists of many microservices. So far, they've used kubectl commands to list services, deployments, and pods. The frontend deployment has two pods, but one is in a Pending state. The user wants to find out why that pod isn't running.\n\nThe next step is to check the details of the frontend pod that's pending. The reasoning is that using `kubectl describe pod` will give more information about the pod's status, such as events, resource constraints, image pull failures, or scheduling issues. This command provides a detailed view of the pod's lifecycle, including any errors or warnings that might indicate why it's not scheduled.\n\nSo, the tool call should be to execute `kubectl describe pod frontend-789b89cfd4-86xlh -n hotel-reservation`. This will retrieve the detailed description of the problematic pod, helping to identify the root cause of the issue. The user needs to check the output for any clues like node taints, resource limits, image pull errors, or other events that might be preventing the pod from starting. Once they have that information, they can proceed to troubleshoot accordingly.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe pod frontend-789b89cfd4-86xlh -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9555bb3272fbc12e', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 306, 'prompt_tokens': 5021, 'total_tokens': 5327, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbe2-e5d9-7062-b3fa-19fb2a30abd1-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl describe pod frontend-789b89cfd4-86xlh -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9555bb3272fbc12e', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5021, 'output_tokens': 306, 'total_tokens': 5327}), ToolMessage(content="Name:             frontend-789b89cfd4-86xlh\nNamespace:        hotel-reservation\nPriority:         0\nService Account:  default\nNode:             <none>\nLabels:           io.kompose.service=frontend\n                  pod-template-hash=789b89cfd4\nAnnotations:      kompose.cmd: kompose convert\n                  kompose.version: 1.22.0 (955b78124)\n                  sidecar.istio.io/statsInclusionPrefixes:\n                    cluster.outbound,cluster_manager,listener_manager,http_mixer_filter,tcp_mixer_filter,server,cluster.xds-grp,listener,connection_manager\n                  sidecar.istio.io/statsInclusionRegexps: http.*\nStatus:           Pending\nIP:               \nIPs:              <none>\nControlled By:    ReplicaSet/frontend-789b89cfd4\nContainers:\n  hotel-reserv-frontend:\n    Image:      yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest\n    Port:       5000/TCP\n    Host Port:  0/TCP\n    Command:\n      frontend\n    Limits:\n      cpu:  1\n    Requests:\n      cpu:  100m\n    Environment:\n      JAEGER_SAMPLE_RATIO:  1\n    Mounts:\n      /frontend-data from frontend-volume (rw)\n      /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount from kube-api-access-rqlsp (ro)\nConditions:\n  Type           Status\n  PodScheduled   False \nVolumes:\n  frontend-volume:\n    Type:       PersistentVolumeClaim (a reference to a PersistentVolumeClaim in the same namespace)\n    ClaimName:  frontend-pvc\n    ReadOnly:   false\n  kube-api-access-rqlsp:\n    Type:                    Projected (a volume that contains injected data from multiple sources)\n    TokenExpirationSeconds:  3607\n    ConfigMapName:           kube-root-ca.crt\n    Optional:                false\n    DownwardAPI:             true\nQoS Class:                   Burstable\nNode-Selectors:              <none>\nTolerations:                 node.kubernetes.io/not-ready:NoExecute op=Exists for 300s\n                             node.kubernetes.io/unreachable:NoExecute op=Exists for 300s\nEvents:\n  Type     Reason            Age                From               Message\n  ----     ------            ----               ----               -------\n  Warning  FailedScheduling  43s (x3 over 51s)  default-scheduler  0/3 nodes are available: 1 node(s) didn't match pod anti-affinity rules, 2 node(s) didn't find available persistent volumes to bind. preemption: 0/3 nodes are available: 1 No preemption victims found for incoming pod, 2 Preemption is not helpful for scheduling.\n  Warning  FailedScheduling  38s                default-scheduler  0/3 nodes are available: 1 node(s) didn't match pod anti-affinity rules, 2 node(s) had volume node affinity conflict. preemption: 0/3 nodes are available: 1 No preemption victims found for incoming pod, 2 Preemption is not helpful for scheduling.\n", id='2b71d705-3228-4b7b-a0ca-df0579fb2c42', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-9555bb3272fbc12e'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='942f93ca-e653-4f44-a56c-2196c9b09f65')]]
